m04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"a815cdc11fd8ead16206089551db115ad308f8579386ab009d059e03c1049b67","compact":"cb23373e04f39c17ddc568762932835f4a0c1a4e2f2555191640477962f1aefe","nasal":"38e6b15212ae384a4c749c6946683f315be13071a7edfe721812528ea4e2a57c"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-66-37","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":66,"verse":37,"english":"Then he equalling the Wind, that monkey, the son of the Wind-god, pounder of enemies, ascended that best of mountains, Mahendra; covered with with various trees and flowers, furnished with swards; ranged by deer; containing plants and blossoms with trees bearing fruits and flowers daily; having tigers and lions and infuriated elephants; swarming with maddened birds; and abounding with fountains. Ascending (Mahendra), that foremost of monkeys endued with exceeding strength, and resembling Mahendra himself in prowess, began to range from one mighty summit to another.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.776,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-67-31"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4066037","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"5d206a2dd494b77a91bf0ae1c7b46d7cdc6d81558475129654a607f2756d4288","compact":"a45ba96bc3c7cd441ec0eac738a3964826aecead950e001ef4970ecde4d25516","nasal":"49e37ded41d178cf561fcd68a1081e1cc63366e4dc7f0154b14452a9a8f0216d"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-66-38","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":66,"verse":38,"english":"Thereat, hurt by the arms of that high-souled one, the mighty mountain began to cry,* like a mighty mad elephant tormented by a lion. *Through the voices of the animals inhabiting it.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.931,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-67-32"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4066038","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"dcb1c1afa87824695066b3e75570675e9c83ae0bf3d9e2dac1c0357dbfe8cb9f","compact":"191b5eabfc50a3adb34bd3de27c83e35357c9f7ae6e3029206bbc0f8fcc99ffe","nasal":"191b5eabfc50a3adb34bd3de27c83e35357c9f7ae6e3029206bbc0f8fcc99ffe"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-66-39","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":66,"verse":39,"english":"And water rushed out of masses of rocks scattered around. And that mighty mountain had its deer and elephants afflicted with affright; and its giant trees shaken; and its spacious uplands deserted by various Gandharva couples engaged in drinking and dalliance, and by birds-flying away, and by bevies of Vidyadharas; and its huge serpents distressed, and its cliffs and peaks toppling down.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-67-33"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4066039","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"cad8ae19c4ca97a7a84f695f5f55d8cdd4bb4ffe1bfae409903b06d34e92fd47","compact":"47282f6c57935b57b2a4a5cceb34b98a32973031293aebc4c66e8d835a98083e","nasal":"346eb6903a6997887e71f18fe3ed6fe76857216b9a1afa877c4610d2a887c086"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-66-40","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":66,"verse":40,"english":"And water rushed out of masses of rocks scattered around. And that mighty mountain had its deer and elephants afflicted with affright; and its giant trees shaken; and its spacious uplands deserted by various Gandharva couples engaged in drinking and dalliance, and by birds-flying away, and by bevies of Vidyadharas; and its huge serpents distressed, and its cliffs and peaks toppling down.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.869,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-67-33"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4066040","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"98357a851bd6b0a175806a978170e05d83743a817b5690e957b5373c5c83c4a9","com